    {
      "id": "Xref",
      "code_hash": "56ed6389d6537b3f246e7230248bebd5",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><span class=\"paragraph\"><strong>Massive angular-cut rates</strong></span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Write <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(\\rho=m^2/s||)</marimo-tex>, with <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(0\\leq\\rho&lt;1||)</marimo-tex>, and <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(z=\\cos\\theta||)</marimo-tex> for the outgoing\nquark angle. Then <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(t=-s(1-\\rho)^2(1-z)/2||)</marimo-tex> and <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(u=2m^2-s-t||)</marimo-tex>.\nNative flux and two-body phase space give</span>\n<marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||[\\frac{d\\sigma}{dz}=\\frac{\\overline{|\\mathcal M|^2}}{32\\pi s}.||]</marimo-tex><span class=\"paragraph\">The quark and gluon are distinct final particles. The cut <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(|z|&lt;c&lt;1||)</marimo-tex> excludes\nthe forward pole and the additional backward pole in the massless limit.</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Symbolica integrates the angular density exactly. Differentiation, cut\nboundary checks, and 27 Gaussian quadratures validate the rates below.\nDisplayed rates use <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(s\\sigma/\\alpha_s^2||)</marimo-tex>, where <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(g_s^2=4\\pi\\alpha_s||)</marimo-tex>.</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
